 ## Background
-The concept of representing scenes with explicit Gaussian primitives originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting, which demonstrated that a large number of anisotropic 3D Gaussians could be rendered in real-time using a custom differentiable rasterizer [S1, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include various geometric extensions, conditional formulations, and hybrid representations to address specific limitations such as boundary artifacts, sparse view reconstruction, and explicit surface extraction [S1, unofficial]. These methods are primarily used in novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction from images, and dynamic scene modeling.
+The concept of Gaussian primitives for volume rendering originated with the introduction of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [S1, unofficial]. The field has rapidly evolved to address limitations in geometry representation, including the introduction of 2D Gaussian Splatting for surface rendering, Half-Gaussians for handling discontinuities, and hybrid representations like Bézier Gaussians for sharp boundaries [S1, unofficial]. Recent developments also include feed-forward methods like AnySplat that generate primitives directly from uncalibrated image collections [S1, unofficial].
 
